"""Reading-first reflection and plausible quiz alternatives for remaining chapters."""
WRONG={
4:[('A comment translating each assignment into English','A change-history note copied from version control'),('Keep both descriptions so readers can choose','Add another comment elsewhere without resolving the contradiction'),('A permanent comment beside every changed line','A naming prefix that records the last modification'),('Keep the condition cryptic and rely only on its comment','Rename unrelated variables without clarifying the condition'),('Comments have no legitimate purpose','Commented code is a reliable record of which behavior is still supported')],
5:[('Minimize file length even when concepts become crowded','Demonstrate that behavior is correct'),('Inside every expression regardless of meaning','Only at the end of a file'),('Only the total number of lines in the file','Only whether all functions have the same length'),('Let each function use its author’s preferred convention','Change conventions whenever a file is edited'),('Correct implementation of the product requirements','Absence of unnecessary dependencies')],
6:[('Yes, access modifiers alone establish a useful abstraction','Only when every accessor has a matching setter'),('An interface that requires callers to know the stored units','A proof that the underlying representation can never change'),('Which form produces the fewest visible keywords','Whether an editor generates accessors automatically'),('Whether every accessor has a short name','Whether private fields alone remove the dependency'),('Apply all domain behavior while exposing no data','Represent every object’s full internal implementation')],
7:[('Replace the cause with a generic message at every boundary','Treat every failure as an ordinary missing result'),('Expose each library exception unchanged to every caller','Catch all failures and return the same empty value'),('It gives callers a reliable way to identify an outage','It guarantees the caller can retry safely'),('They make every missing case self-explanatory','They remove the need to define an absence contract'),('Capturing the cause along with operation context','Giving the caller an explicit failure contract')],
8:[('Reproduce the library’s complete interface throughout the app','Assume a stable library will never require adaptation'),('A test that predicts every future library change','A replacement for all tests of the application’s own behavior'),('An implementation based on guessed undocumented responses','An interface that exposes every possible supplier feature'),('Force every caller to translate the vendor’s error format','Add unrelated application state to the vendor client'),('Only formatting checks on the dependency declaration','Only whether the old code still imports successfully')],
9:[('Depend on the previous test’s setup','Share mutable fixtures whose state depends on run order'),('Fast','Repeatable'),('A passing result makes the protected behavior obvious','Only the production implementation needs readable intent'),('Enforce one assertion even when it fragments one coherent behavior','Group unrelated behaviors because they use the same setup'),('Only the most common successful input','Only internal helper names and formatting')],
10:[('A fixed maximum line count regardless of behavior','One public method regardless of the responsibility'),('Only whether all fields use the same type','Only whether the constructor can initialize every field'),('Every class with two methods must be split','All operations on customer information must change together'),('Direct construction of every concrete dependency inside each operation','Sharing mutable global dependencies to reduce parameters'),('Yes, if each new class reduces a line count','No class should ever delegate to another class')],
11:[('Remove visible parameters while hiding all wiring in globals','Require every business method to construct its own dependencies'),('A guarantee that the dependency has no implementation details','A requirement to choose a particular framework'),('A responsibility that necessarily belongs in every domain method','A concern solved only by changing identifier names'),('Whenever it is the most widely mentioned option','Before understanding the system’s constraints'),('Prove that no architectural decision will ever change','Replace every lower-level behavior check')],
12:[('Minimize the number of classes before checking behavior','Remove duplication before establishing a passing baseline'),('It merges similar syntax despite different policies','It reduces a line count without revealing intent'),('Fewer lines always mean fewer responsibilities','Formatting alone determines whether the design is clear'),('The exact current arrangement of helper methods','The spelling of every internal variable'),('Prohibit helpers even when they clarify a responsibility','Optimize class counts before checking expression and duplication')],
13:[('The two reads make the entire operation atomic','Both increments are guaranteed to be retained'),('Yes, synchronization of each call protects every sequence','Only when the method names describe the sequence'),('Passing runs prove every schedule is safe','A failure can be ignored if most runs pass'),('Broaden every critical section to the entire application','Dismiss failures that are difficult to reproduce'),('Assume every library terminates waiting work automatically','Treat outstanding work as unrelated to termination')],
14:[('Memorize the exact arrangement of every helper class','Replace the parser without establishing its existing behavior'),('Only whether the new type uses the same naming prefix','Only whether the added branch fits on one screen'),('Only compilation after the names change','Only the shortest successful command line'),('A smaller diff automatically proves equivalent behavior','A refactoring must introduce another supported argument type'),('The extraction increases the number of classes','The extracted code has fewer characters regardless of meaning')],
17:[('An automatic mandate to rewrite the whole module','A substitute for investigating observed behavior'),('Another literal chosen to be easier to remember','A comment that leaves the underlying meaning unspecified'),('Only whether the dependent operation has a short name','Only whether the calling function fits on one screen'),('Exercise only the most common successful value','Check only that the input type is accepted'),('Apply Java import conventions literally to TypeScript','Ignore every general design principle because the example is Java')],
}
REFLECTION={
4:('Compare a useful explanatory comment with one that repeats the code. What information does each add? Explain when a clearer name or helper would remove the need for the second comment.','A useful comment supplies reasoning, constraints or warnings that code alone does not express. Repeating an obvious operation adds maintenance work without much information. A clear name can express intent, while a real external constraint may still need explanation.'),
5:('Compare the formatting examples. Explain how spacing, proximity and ordering help a reader distinguish concepts. Which choices should the team agree on?','Spacing separates concepts; proximity and ordering help readers follow related work. Team conventions reduce avoidable visual differences. Passing a formatter establishes mechanical consistency, not behavioral correctness.'),
6:('Compare an interface exposing stored fuel measurements with one reporting percent remaining. What does the caller need to know in each case? How could a future representation change affect it?','An interface can expose storage details despite using private fields and accessors. A domain operation can hide those details when it matches the caller’s needs. Objects and data structures favor different extension patterns; choose based on the changes and operations required.'),
7:('Explain the difference between no matching lesson and a failed lesson service. What should a caller learn from each result, and what context would help with a failure?','Ordinary absence and service failure have different meanings. Communicate the contract explicitly, preserve useful failure context and avoid making an outage look like valid empty data. The error-handling mechanism also depends on the language and caller.'),
8:('Choose an external-library example from the chapter. Which operations does the application actually need? Which vendor details should stay at the boundary, and which behaviors deserve learning tests?','A focused boundary expresses application needs and contains vendor-specific formats or errors. Learning tests explore the external behavior the application relies on and can reveal relevant changes after an upgrade.'),
9:('Read a test example and explain its protected behavior without narrating every line. What makes the setup, action and result understandable? Which boundary or failure case would you want clarified?','Readable tests communicate one coherent behavior, arrange the required state and produce an automated result. Independence and repeatability matter. Relevant boundary and failure cases supplement the common successful case; assertion count alone does not determine focus.'),
10:('Inspect the class examples. Which fields and methods belong to the same responsibility? What changes would affect them, and where might a dependency boundary help?','Look for related state and behavior and reasons to change together. Separate independent responsibilities when that improves understanding. A useful boundary permits substitution and focused verification; creating the greatest number of classes is not the goal.'),
11:('Explain how constructing an application differs from using it. In one chapter example, which wiring or infrastructure details could be kept out of business behavior?','Construction chooses and connects dependencies; business operations use them. Explicit boundaries can contain wiring and cross-cutting policies. Evaluate architecture techniques against actual needs and keep decisions revisable rather than assuming one framework solves every system.'),
12:('Explain the order of the simple-design priorities. Why would removing classes or duplication before understanding behavior sometimes make the design worse?','Start with passing behavior checks, then refine duplication and expression and avoid unnecessary entities. An extraction should express a genuinely shared concept. A lower class or line count is not proof of clarity or correctness.'),
13:('Trace how two workers could lose an increment. Then explain how ownership, copies or coordination might change the situation. Why do repeated passing tests leave uncertainty?','Two workers can read the same old value and overwrite one another’s update. Clear ownership, independent copies or a suitable atomic/coordinated operation can address the contract. Passing executions do not cover every possible schedule. Java threads and JavaScript async mechanisms differ.'),
14:('Compare the parser’s draft and refined versions. Identify one responsibility that became clearer and explain which behavior checks would support that change. You do not need to reproduce the listings.','A useful explanation identifies a concrete improvement in responsibility, duplication or naming and preserves the parser’s contract. Check valid arguments, missing values and relevant conversion failures through small changes; a shorter listing alone is not sufficient evidence.'),
17:('Choose one smell from the chapter. What observable problem would make it relevant, what improvement might address it, and what evidence would you check before accepting that change?','Treat a smell as a prompt for investigation rather than a verdict. Explain the context, consequence and tradeoff, then check affected behavior. Separate language-specific recommendations from transferable ideas.'),
}

def apply(material):
    for chapter,alternatives in WRONG.items():
        original=material[chapter]['questions'];questions=[]
        for index,((prompt,options,answer,explanation),wrong) in enumerate(zip(original,alternatives)):
            correct=options[answer]
            if chapter==14 and index==3:
                prompt='A parser refactoring changes internal names and structure. What distinguishes it from adding a feature?'
                correct='Existing observable behavior is preserved'
                explanation='Refactoring improves internal structure while preserving the existing contract. Adding a supported option or argument type changes capability and needs its own behavior checks.'
            questions.append((prompt,[correct,*wrong],0,explanation))
        assert len(questions)==len(original)==5
        material[chapter]['questions']=questions
        prompt,check=REFLECTION[chapter]
        material[chapter]['practice']=prompt+' Answer in your notes; no code editing is required.'
        material[chapter]['check']=check
